# November 19, 2026: southern Berkshire County, Massachusetts (Sheffield, Great Barrington, Egremont, Stockbridge,
# West Stockbridge, Monterey, Tyringham, Sandisfield). Drawn at random by tools/choose.py (region berk).
# Sources read in this session: Wikipedia articles through tools/wiki_raw.py; HMdb marker pages through tools/page.py;
# the managing bodies' own pages (The Trustees, the Bidwell House Museum, the Du Bois Homesite's site duboisnhs.org,
# Chesterwood) through tools/page.py; a Massachusetts DCR event flyer for Jug End read with the web fetch tool.
# Pins: ArcGIS geocoder PointAddress results, HMdb marker positions and OpenStreetMap points, each looked at on the
# MassGIS 2023 leaf-off aerial with tools/pincheck.py.
from data_b import N
def W(t): return ['Wikipedia: '+t,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/'+t.replace(' ','_')]
def L(a,b): return [a,b]
def HM(n,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=%d'%n]
BATCH={'key':'berk_vhiw','label':'Southern Berkshires, Massachusetts','added':'2026-11-19 04:49'}
TRUST='The Trustees (thetrustees.org)'
PLACES=[
N('ashley-house-sheffield','Colonel John Ashley House','Sheffield','berk','civic','standing',42.05943,-73.35564,'exact','open','walk','','1735',
  'Built in 1735 by John Ashley, a militia colonel and enslaver, it is one of the oldest houses in southern Berkshire County. The Sheffield Declaration of 1773 was drafted in its upstairs study. Elizabeth "Mum Bett" Freeman, enslaved in this household, won her freedom in court in 1781 in the case that helped end slavery in Massachusetts. The house was moved about three tenths of a mile in 1930.',
  look='A five-bay clapboard house with a central chimney and an elaborate doorway: pilasters under a broken pediment. Inside are 18th- and early 19th-century furnishings (by guided tour only). An outdoor exhibit on Elizabeth Freeman stands on the grounds, and a trail crosses Ashley Field to Bartholomew\'s Cobble.',
  accn='Owned and run by The Trustees. Its page (read November 2026) says the grounds and the Elizabeth Freeman exhibit are open daily, sunrise to sunset, year-round; the house is seen by guided tour only and is closed for tours until spring 2027. Parking for about 10 cars at the entrance on Cooper Hill Road; the Trustees ask that you not park along the road.',
  links=[W('Colonel John Ashley House'),L('The Trustees: Ashley House','https://thetrustees.org/place/ashley-house/')],
  th=[42.05933,-73.35666],thn='Ashley House lot, Cooper Hill Road',brick='no'),
N('bidwell-house-monterey','Bidwell House','Monterey','berk','civic','standing',42.20838,-73.21823,'exact','open','walk','','c. 1750–1760s',
  'The saltbox home of the Reverend Adonijah Bidwell, first minister of Township No. 1 (now Monterey and Tyringham), who served from 1750 until his death in 1784; the house dates to about 1750 by its Register marker and the 1760s by the museum and Wikipedia. Bidwells lived here until 1853. Two New York designers bought it in 1960 and spent some 25 years restoring it, and it opened as a museum in 1990.',
  look='A Georgian saltbox around a central chimney with four fireplaces and two beehive ovens, the chimney carried on a stone arch in the cellar; an 1820s ell and an 1840s Greek Revival carriage-barn addition. Furnished with period pieces, many matched to the Reverend\'s estate inventory. The 192-acre grounds hold original proprietors\' lots, cellar holes, stone walls and a stretch of the old Boston-Albany Post Road, on about six miles of trails.',
  accn='Run by the Bidwell House Museum. Its pages (read November 2026) say the grounds, gardens and trails are open every day, year-round, during daylight hours, free; leashed dogs welcome; no hunting, no metal detectors. House tours by appointment; a marker at the lot gives Memorial Day to October on Monday, Thursday, Friday and Saturday. Main parking lot on Art School Road with a trail kiosk.',
  links=[W('Bidwell House Museum'),L('Bidwell House Museum','https://www.bidwellhousemuseum.org/'),L('Bidwell House Museum: Trails','https://www.bidwellhousemuseum.org/trails'),HM(240852,'Welcome to the Bidwell House Museum')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.20717,-73.21865],thn='Bidwell House Museum main lot, Art School Road',brick='?'),
N('township-one-meeting-house-site','First Meeting House site of Township No. 1','Monterey','berk','worship','traces',42.20549,-73.21864,'site','public','walk','','1740s–1790s',
  'The first meeting house of Township No. 1, built in the 1740s on proprietors\' Lot 1 and used until the 1790s. Settlers from the Hop Brook valley (now Tyringham village) climbed a steep two-mile road over the ridge to attend services here. The lot is now part of the Bidwell House Museum\'s land.',
  look='The site lies on the Meeting House Trail, which heads south from the kiosk at the museum\'s parking lot. What survives on the ground was not checked in a source; OpenStreetMap maps the site, and an old spring, a barn foundation, cellar holes, a sugar house chimney and a charcoal hearth close by.',
  accn='Bidwell House Museum land: grounds and trails open every day, year-round, during daylight hours, free; leashed dogs welcome; no hunting, no metal detectors (museum\'s trail page, read November 2026). Park at the museum lot on Art School Road.',
  links=[L('Bidwell House Museum: Trails (Lot #1, first meeting house)','https://www.bidwellhousemuseum.org/trails')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.20717,-73.21865],thn='Bidwell House Museum main lot (Meeting House Trailhead)',brick='?'),
N('du-bois-boyhood-homesite','W. E. B. Du Bois Boyhood Homesite','Great Barrington','berk','town','ruins',42.17801,-73.39467,'site','public','walk','','1700s–1954',
  'The farm of the "Black Burghardts", the family of W. E. B. Du Bois\'s mother, on the edge of the Egremont Plain; Du Bois lived in his grandfather\'s house here for his first five years. He was given the house in 1928 and hoped to restore it, but sold it in 1954, and it was demolished. The site was dedicated as a memorial in 1969, made a National Historic Landmark in 1976 and given to the state in 1987, with the University of Massachusetts Amherst as custodian; its field schools have dug here since 1983.',
  look='A wood-chip interpretive trail runs from the lot through pines to the 1969 commemorative boulder and the stone foundation of the family house. Stakes mark the outline of the house, with the remains of the chimney Du Bois had built for a summer cottage he never finished.',
  accn='State land with UMass Amherst as custodian. The Homesite\'s page says it is open daily from dawn to dusk for self-guided visits; its other page says spring, summer and fall. Parking lot at 612 South Egremont Road (Routes 23/41) big enough for a school bus.',
  links=[W('W. E. B. Du Bois Boyhood Homesite'),L('Du Bois Homesite (duboisnhs.org)','https://www.duboisnhs.org/w-e-b-du-bois-homesite/'),L('Du Bois Homesite: Visit','https://www.duboisnhs.org/visit/'),HM(194758,'The House of the Black Burghardts')],
  th=[42.1776,-73.39337],thn='Du Bois Homesite lot, South Egremont Road',brick='?'),
N('mission-house-stockbridge','Mission House','Stockbridge','berk','civic','standing',42.28345,-73.31583,'site','open','drive','','1741–1742',
  'Built in 1741 and 1742 by John Sergeant, the first missionary to the Mahicans of the Stockbridge mission, on the hill above the town. It stayed in his family until the 1870s. In the 1920s Mabel Choate of Naumkeag bought it and had it moved down into the valley, where she and Fletcher Steele restored and furnished it; it opened as a museum in 1930 and is a National Historic Landmark.',
  look='A colonial house furnished with 18th-century pieces, with a garden laid out by Fletcher Steele. An exhibit by the Stockbridge-Munsee Community tells the Mohican side, and the Footprints of our Ancestors walking tour of Main Street starts here. Interior detail not checked.',
  accn='Owned by The Trustees. Its page (read November 2026) says the garden is open daily, year-round; the house and exhibition are closed until spring 2027, with guided tours in season for a fee. The red barn behind is the Trustees\' Berkshires office. Parking not checked: OpenStreetMap maps public lots off Main Street and Pine Street nearby.',
  links=[W('Mission House (Stockbridge, Massachusetts)'),L('The Trustees: The Mission House','https://www.thetrustees.org/place/the-mission-house')],
  brick='?'),
N('naumkeag-stockbridge','Naumkeag','Stockbridge','berk','civic','standing',42.2897,-73.31606,'site','open','drive','','1885–1886',
  'The summer estate of the New York lawyer and ambassador Joseph Hodges Choate, a 44-room Shingle Style house designed mostly by Stanford White and built in 1885 and 1886. Its daughter Mabel worked with the landscape designer Fletcher Steele from 1926 to 1956 on gardens that include the Blue Steps (1938). She left it to The Trustees in 1958 to stay as it was; it is a National Historic Landmark District.',
  look='The house with its Gilded Age contents, and the Afternoon Garden, Blue Steps and Chinese Garden: one of only two Fletcher Steele gardens left and the only one open to the public.',
  accn='Owned and run by The Trustees at 5 Prospect Hill Road. Its page (read November 2026) says the house and grounds are closed to general admission and guided tours have ended for the year; hours vary by season, tickets are booked ahead for the fall, winter and spring events, and house tours cost extra. A lot on Prospect Hill Road by the house.',
  links=[W('Naumkeag'),L('The Trustees: Naumkeag','https://thetrustees.org/place/naumkeag/')],
  th=[42.28991,-73.31715],thn='Naumkeag lot, Prospect Hill Road',brick='?'),
N('chesterwood-stockbridge','Chesterwood','Stockbridge','berk','civic','standing',42.28563,-73.35166,'site','open','drive','','1896–1931',
  'The summer home and studio of the sculptor Daniel Chester French, who bought the farm in 1896. His friend Henry Bacon designed the studio and, in 1901, the Georgian Revival house that replaced the farmhouse. French did his preliminary work for the Lincoln Memorial statue here, including a 6-foot model. The National Trust for Historic Preservation runs it; it is a National Historic Landmark.',
  look='The studio, the restored Colonial Revival house, a 19th-century barn now a gallery, the gardens with a Bacon fountain, and a woodland walk French laid out.',
  accn='National Trust for Historic Preservation, 4 Williamsville Road. Its page (read November 2026): May 15 to October 31, 2026, open daily except Tuesday, 10 am to 5 pm; December 4 to 20, 2026, Friday to Sunday 11 am to 4 pm. Admission charged; the house and studio by guided tour, which often sells out. Parking area on the grounds.',
  links=[W('Chesterwood (Massachusetts)'),L('Chesterwood: Hours and Admission','https://www.chesterwood.org/visit')],
  brick='?',hours='May 15 to October 31, 2026: daily except Tuesday, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. December 4 to 20, 2026: Friday to Sunday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('old-covered-bridge-sheffield','Old Covered Bridge (Upper Sheffield Covered Bridge)','Sheffield','berk','landmark','standing',42.12395,-73.35456,'exact','public','walk','','1837 or 1854; rebuilt 1996',
  'A Town lattice-truss covered bridge over the Housatonic, once called the oldest covered bridge in Massachusetts. Wikipedia dates it to 1837, while the town\'s marker says it was ordered built in 1854. It was closed to traffic in 1974 (the marker; Wikipedia gives 1970), restored in 1981 by Milton Graton, burned by arsonists in 1994, and rebuilt to the restoration drawings, finished in 1996.',
  look='What stands is the 1996 replica: a 93-foot board-sided bridge with two lattice trusses on granite and Sheffield marble abutments, one of them 19th-century. Open to people on foot. The marker stands by the bridge.',
  accn='On Covered Bridge Lane, off Route 7 north of Sheffield center; Wikipedia says the bridge is open to pedestrians. Who owns it was not checked. OpenStreetMap maps a small lot at the west end, an open patch beside the lane on the aerial.',
  links=[W('Old Covered Bridge'),HM(37504,'Reconstruction of 1854 Covered Bridge')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.12339,-73.35484],thn='Covered Bridge Lane, west end',brick='no'),
N('old-parish-church-sheffield','Old Parish Church','Sheffield','berk','worship','standing',42.1105,-73.35368,'exact','view','drive','','1760',
  'Sheffield built this meeting house in 1760 near its present spot to be central to the town, and the village center grew round it. The clock was placed by the Grand Army of the Republic as a memorial to the composer George F. Root, born in Sheffield in 1820.',
  look='A white meeting house on Main Street in the Sheffield Center historic district, with the memorial clock, a cast-steel bell and an Elizabeth Freeman marker in front. Interior not checked.',
  accn='A church on Route 7 (Main Street) at Elm Court; seen from the street. Whether and when the public may go in was not checked.',
  links=[W('Sheffield Center Historic District'),HM(58801,'Old Parish Church Clock')],
  brick='?'),
N('monument-mills-housatonic','Monument Mills','Great Barrington','berk','industry','standing',42.25482,-73.36514,'site','view','drive','','1850–1955',
  'Textile mills along both banks of the Housatonic in Housatonic village, worked from 1850; the second mill (1866) made jacquard bedspreads and the first company bought it. The mills closed in 1955 and fell into vacancy and neglect. The company also built the Glendale Power House upstream in 1905.',
  look='Mill No. 1 between the railroad and the west bank at the north end of the village, and Mill No. 2 on the east bank above the Park Street bridge. Wikipedia lists a 50-foot turbine room with a gantry, a roofless boiler house with 30-foot windows and a tall water tower. Parts house artists and small businesses; a Berkshire Edge article calls the mills abandoned and vacant (its date was not shown on the page read). A freight line runs through.',
  accn='Private property. Seen from Park Street, Front Street and the bridge; do not enter. The railroad through the complex is active.',
  links=[W('Monument Mills'),L('The Berkshire Edge: Is there still a redeveloped mill complex in Housatonic\'s future?','https://theberkshireedge.com/is-there-still-a-redeveloped-mill-complex-in-housatonics-future/')],
  brick='?'),
N('rising-paper-mill','Rising Paper Mill','Great Barrington','berk','industry','standing',42.24091,-73.3579,'site','view','drive','','c. 1873',
  'Built about 1873 for H. D. Cone, who meant it to be the world\'s largest paper mill and went bankrupt soon after. It stood empty until about 1900, when the B. D. Rising Company made paper here; it became the area\'s biggest employer after Monument Mills failed. Hazen Paper bought it in 2008.',
  look='Two long three-story brick blocks (212 and 145 feet) joined by a narrower middle section, under mansard slate roofs with towers at the corners, granite quoins from Monument Mountain and bands of Greek crosses under the eaves. The dam is at the north end.',
  accn='Private industrial property at 295 Park Street North (Route 183); whether it still runs was not checked. Seen from the road only.',
  links=[W('Rising Paper Mill')],brick='yes'),
N('glendale-power-house','Glendale Power House','Stockbridge','berk','industry','standing',42.28111,-73.35206,'exact','view','drive','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 by Monument Mills at the site of an 1840s paper-mill dam, one of the first plants in the country to make electricity to run a factory; by 1909 it supplied half the mills\' power. It was shut in 1946 and most of its machinery removed, then rebuilt in the 1980s to feed the grid with four turbines.',
  look='A one-story stone power house with a slate roof, built of stone quarried for the Glendale Woolen Mill, at the foot of a 1,500-foot power canal; the 28-foot concrete dam is at the north end, below the Glendale Middle Road bridge.',
  accn='A working private hydro plant beside Route 183. Seen from the road and the bridge; do not enter.',
  links=[W('Glendale Power House')],brick='no'),
N('clinton-ame-zion-church','Clinton A.M.E. Zion Church','Great Barrington','berk','worship','standing',42.19652,-73.36272,'exact','view','walk','','1886–1887',
  'The first African American church in Berkshire County, a Shingle Style building dedicated in 1887 for a congregation that had gathered since the 1860s. W. E. B. Du Bois, whose uncle was active in it, attended services. The church closed in 2014; Clinton Church Restoration bought it and is restoring it as a center on Du Bois and Black history in the Berkshires.',
  look='A shingled church with decorative gable shingles and a 30-foot corner tower. Inside (as listed in 2008): twenty pews, windows with some stained glass, an ash pulpit; the original wainscot is covered by paneling. The 1938 parsonage adjoins. How much of the interior survives the restoration was not checked.',
  accn='Owned by Clinton Church Restoration, 9 Elm Court, a short walk from Main Street. Seen from the street; whether it is open was not checked (its own site would not open).',
  links=[W('Clinton African Methodist Episcopal Zion Church'),L('The Berkshire Edge: What\'s happening with the Clinton Church Restoration?','https://theberkshireedge.com/its-not-that-simple-whats-happening-with-the-clinton-church-restoration/')],
  brick='no'),
N('jug-end-barn-site','Jug End Barn resort site','Egremont','berk','hotel','traces',42.14839,-73.44988,'area','public','hike','','1935–1983',
  'A 1928 cattle barn turned into a hotel in 1935, Jug End Barn grew into one of the best-known Berkshire resorts, with ski trails on Mount Sterling from 1936, skating, pools and a bowling alley. DCR gives 1935 to 1983 as its years. The state took the land, and the reservation has been let go back to field and forest.',
  look='A DCR flyer says the barn is gone and "only scant traces remain". A hiking blog says a silo stands in the parking lot; a ski-history site describes a circular foundation above the gate and the old ski trail cuts. Not checked on the ground. The 2-mile Jug End Loop Trail and the Appalachian Trail cross the reservation.',
  accn='Jug End State Reservation and Wildlife Management Area, Massachusetts DCR. Parking lot on Jug End Road (about 116 Jug End Road, Egremont, per the DCR flyer). Ticks in the tall grass. Hours, dog rules and hunting seasons not checked: DCR\'s page could not be read.',
  links=[W('Jug End State Reservation and Wildlife Management Area'),L('Mass. DCR flyer: Jug End, from resort to refuge','https://www.mass.gov/doc/jug-end-from-resort-to-refuge-september-23/download'),L('NELSAP: Jug End Barn','https://nelsap.org/ma/jugend.html'),L('Western Mass Hilltown Hikers: Jug End Barn','https://westernmasshilltownhikers.com/2022/09/05/jug-end-barn-lost-ski-resort-in-egremont/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('shaker-mill-west-stockbridge','The Shaker Mill','West Stockbridge','berk','industry','standing',42.33465,-73.36819,'exact','view','drive','','1805',
  'In 1805 Enoch Thayer joined two barns moved here to make a grist mill, at a dam where Elijah Williams had worked an iron forge on Mohican land leased in 1763. The Tyringham Shakers bought it in 1818 and ground grain until 1851. It gave the town its first electric lights in 1909, was a hardware and feed store from 1933 and an antiques shop from the 1970s; a bookseller bought it in 2013.',
  look='The old barn-built mill on the Williams River, known locally as The Barn, now showing books and book art where the millstones stood; the riverside power wing is gone.',
  accn='A bookshop at 1 Depot Street, off Route 102; open hours not checked. Seen from the street.',
  links=[HM(307302,'The Shaker Mill')],brick='?'),
N('santarella-tyringham','Santarella','Tyringham','berk','landmark','standing',42.25465,-73.21415,'site','view','drive','','c. 1920',
  'From about 1920 the English-American sculptor Henry Hudson Kitson, maker of the Lexington Minuteman, turned a dairy barn here into a studio he called Santarella, known as the Gingerbread House. He added a cottage and a tower and worked on it for the last 25 years of his life. It later held the Tyringham Galleries, a museum and a wedding venue.',
  look='The studio\'s 80-ton rolling roof of asphalt shingles, meant to look like autumn leaves in the wind, with big stone buttresses, stained glass and Gothic doorways; the cottage and the detached tower.',
  accn='A private estate at 75 Main Road (Tyringham Road), which hosts private events for a fee. View from the road only; ask before going in.',
  links=[HM(240847,'Santarella'),L('Housatonic Heritage: Santarella Gardens (private estate)','https://housatonicheritage.org/?p=7844362')],
  brick='?'),
N('tyringham-shaker-settlement','Tyringham Shaker settlement','Tyringham','berk','town','standing',42.24594,-73.22088,'area','view','drive','','1792–1870s',
  'A Shaker community founded in 1792 on Jerusalem Road, which at its height held more than 2,000 acres in Tyringham and nearby towns, with mills, workshops and a furnace. It shrank before the Civil War, lost 23 members in 1858, and in the 1870s the last Shakers moved to Hancock and Enfield. The land became a summer boarding house, the short-lived Tyringham Forest Club, and then farmland.',
  look='A historic district of farm buildings on Jerusalem Road. Which Shaker buildings survive was not checked.',
  accn='Private farmland; view from Jerusalem Road only.',
  links=[W('Tyringham Shaker Settlement Historic District')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('sandisfield-arts-center','Montville Baptist Church (Sandisfield Arts Center)','Sandisfield','berk','worship','standing',42.1188,-73.12488,'exact','view','drive','','1839–1842',
  'A Greek Revival church built in 1839 to 1842 by Sandisfield\'s Baptists. It stood empty from 1894 until 1921, when the B\'nai Abraham Orthodox congregation bought it, raised it onto a new foundation and made it a synagogue; that closed in the 1940s. It passed to the Sandisfield Arts Center in 1996.',
  look='A clapboard church with corner pilasters, a full pediment and a two-stage square tower, raised over a granite basement. The upper room is one tall open space, with the old Torah ark against the back wall.',
  accn='Sandisfield Arts Center, 5 Hammertown Road, off Route 57; a venue for events. Seen from the road; open hours not checked (its site would not open).',
  links=[W('Montville Baptist Church'),L('Housatonic Heritage: Sandisfield Arts Center','https://housatonicheritage.org/?p=7844391')],
  brick='no'),
N('mahaiwe-theater','Mahaiwe Theater','Great Barrington','berk','amuse','standing',42.19427,-73.36343,'exact','open','walk','','1905',
  'The Mahaiwe opened on September 26, 1905 in the Mahaiwe Block, designed by Joseph McArthur Vance, with vaudeville, silent films and later talkies; John Philip Sousa\'s band played here in 1912. A nonprofit founded in 2002 raised over $9 million for a restoration led by Hugh Hardy, and it reopened as a performing arts center.',
  look='The restored 1905 theater on Castle Street beside Town Hall. The marker for the first Berkshire court house (1764), where open resistance to British rule took place on August 16, 1774, stands in front of Town Hall next door. Interior detail not checked.',
  accn='Mahaiwe Performing Arts Center, 14 Castle Street; inside for performances (tickets). Downtown street parking.',
  links=[W('Mahaiwe Performing Arts Center'),HM(58958,'The Mahaiwe Theater'),HM(58957,'First Court House of Berkshire County')],
  brick='?'),
N('searles-castle','Searles Castle','Great Barrington','berk','landmark','standing',42.19168,-73.36324,'exact','view','walk','','1880s',
  'A French chateau-style house of the 1880s with seven stories, 40 rooms and 36 fireplaces, commissioned for Mary Hopkins, widow of the Central Pacific Railroad\'s Mark Hopkins; her second husband Edward Searles designed the interior and kept it until 1920. It was later a girls\' school, a company training center, a country club and, from the mid-1980s, the John Dewey Academy. The artist Hunt Slonem bought it in 2021.',
  look='The blue dolomite walls and towers above Main Street, and a cross-shaped pond made to reflect the castle. The carriage house is a replacement for one that burned.',
  accn='Private residence. View from Main Street (Route 7) only.',
  links=[W('Searles Castle (Massachusetts)')],brick='no'),
]
